Historically, a user request travels through three security zones on its way to
a Postfix core daemon process:

           Zone 1            Zone 2             Zone 3
          user UID          user UID         "postfix" UID
          user GID     Set-gid "postdrop"    "postfix" GID

          mail(1)                              pickup(8)
         mailx(1)          postdrop(1)        postlogd(8)
        sendmail(1) ->     postlog(1)     ->    qmgr(8)
         mailq(1)          postqueue(1)        flush(8)
                                               showq(8)

  * Zone 1 contains Non-Postfix utilities such as mail(1) and mailx(1),
    alongside Postfix helper programs such as sendmail(1) and mailq(1) that
    operate without Postfix-specific user or group privileges. Because Zone 1
    processes cannot directly access Postfix queues or other Postfix-internal
    resources, they must request service through Zone 2.

  * Zone 2 contains the maildrop queue and Postfix client processes including
    postdrop(1), postlog(1) and postqueue(1). While the processes retain the
    UID of the invoking user, they run with set-gid "postdrop" group
    privileges. This allows them to access the maildrop queue and specific
    Postfix core daemon processes.

  * Zone 3 contains all other Postfix queues and all core daemon processes.
    Following initialization, these processes run with "postfix" user and group
    privileges. Some daemon processes (not shown in the figure) may temporarily
    impersonate a specific user account when delivering mail or running non-
    Postfix code.

