The setgid-based, three-zone, architecture has several key benefits. These will
need to be preserved on systems that don't support set-gid privileges:

  * Zone 2 can accept email submissions from Zone 1 even when the Postfix
    system is down and no Zone 3 processes are running. This is possible
    because the set-gid privilege allows the postdrop(1) command to deposit
    messages into the protected maildrop queue.

  * Zone 2 enforces a per-client-UID concurrency limit based on the host's per-
    user process limit, as each service request requires a distinct Zone 2 set-
    gid process. However, this may not be an effective deterrent against
    overload, because per-user process limits vary wildly between UNIX-like
    systems.

  * Zone 2 enforces low-level input validation before a request reaches Zone 3.

  * Zone 2 enforcement resists tampering by unprivileged users, because Zone 2
    processes run with distinct UIDs and GIDs from Zone 1.

