Some systems disable set-gid privilege elevation, for example using
"NoNewPrivileges=yes" or the "nosuid" file system mount option. On these
systems, Postfix may use an alternative privilege elevation method that is
implemented with the postlift(8) service, changing the architecture as shown
below. As of Postfix 3.12, this alternative method is supported only on Linux
environments using systemd.

                   Zone 1                       Zone 2               Zone 3
                  user UID                  "postdrop" UID        "postfix" UID
                  user GID                  "postdrop" GID        "postfix" GID

      mail(1)                                                       pickup(8)
     mailx(1)      postdrop(1)                    postdrop(1)      postlogd(8)
    sendmail(1) ->  postlog(1)  -> postlift(8) ->  postlog(1)  ->    qmgr(8)
     mailq(1)      postqueue(1)                   postqueue(1)      flush(8)
                                                                    showq(8)

When set-gid privileges are disabled, a postdrop(1), postlog(1), or postqueue
(1) client process with Zone 1 privileges will connect to the Zone 2 postlift
(8) service, requesting that postlift(8) runs the same command (postdrop(1)
etc.) in a process with Zone 2 privileges. This allows the elevated process to
add a message to the protected maildrop queue, or to interact with a Zone 3
core daemon process. postlift(8) propagates the exit status from the elevated
process back to the Zone 1 client process.

The super-user ("root") is excluded from postlift(8) privilege elevation.
Because root already has sufficient privileges, its Zone 1 postdrop(1), postlog
(1), or postqueue(1) processes can directly access the maildrop queue or a Zone
3 process. This eliminates a postlift(8) start-up dependency and avoids some
implementation complexity.

