The architecture above can provide most of the benefits from the architecture
based on set-gid privilege elevation:

  * (worse) Zone 2 can accept email submissions from Zone 1 while the Postfix
    system is down, under two conditions:

      o Root-privileged processes: accepted at any time.

      o Non-root processes: accepted only after a system start-up target
        activates the postlift(8) socket. Other submissions will be lost.

    Note: some boot-time activity may generate email before a "network" or
    "multi-user" start-up target activates the postlift(8) socket.

  * (better) Zone 2 can enforce a per-client-UID concurrency limit before a
    request reaches Zone 3, relying on systemd or similar infrastructure for
    enforcement instead of host-based process limits.

  * (same) Zone 2 enforces low-level input validation before a request reaches
    Zone 3.

  * (same) Zone 2 enforcement resists tampering by unprivileged users, because
    Zone 2 processes run with distinct UIDs and GIDs from Zone 1.

