On systems without systemd, partial functionality can be implemented using the
Postfix spawn(8) service. This service accepts connections on a private or
public (but still protected) socket, and executes a configured program with
specific privileges. However, making this work with postlift(8) requires two
additional Postfix features:

  * A new master(5) listen socket type, distinct from the existing "private" or
    "public" types. This socket must be world-writable, and hosted in directory
    with world read+execute permission so that it is accessible to unprivileged
    Zone 1 client commands like postdrop(1).

  * spawn(8) integration with the existing anvil(8) service. This integration
    limits the per-userID concurrency for requests originating from Zone 1.

A down-side is that spawn(8) daemons remain unavailable until Postfix starts.
Consequently, Zone 1 email submissions from non-root processes will fail to
enter the queue before Postfix is running. Because Postfix typically launches
with a "network" or "multi-user" start-up target, early boot-time email
messages from non-root users may be dropped.

CCrreeddiittss

  * Thorsten Kukuk proposed a systemd integration for the postdrop(1) command
    which inspired the postlift(8) design.

  * Wietse generalized the approach in multiple dimensions:

      o Multiple client support: one postlift(8) configuration can elevate
        privileges for postdrop(1), postlog(1) and postqueue(1).

      o Multiple instance support: one postlift(8) configuration serves
        multiple Postfix instances that are managed with postmulti(1).

